<%!-- A draft, written from what the code actually does. It describes the three
      third parties that run on the site, the two cookies Ethos sets itself,
      and how to refuse the first group. It makes no claim about any
      jurisdiction, and the contact line names no address because none has been
      supplied — see the task report. --%>
<div class="mx-auto max-w-2xl px-4 py-10">
  <header>
    <h1 class="text-3xl font-bold">Privacy</h1>
    <p class="mt-1 text-zinc-500">
      What Ethos collects, which third parties run on the site, and how to refuse them.
    </p>
  </header>

  <section class="mt-8">
    <h2 class="font-semibold">Third parties that run on this site</h2>
    <p class="mt-3 text-zinc-700">
      Three companies run code in your browser when you read a guide. Each one is here for
      a different reason, and each one can see something different.
    </p>

    <h3 class="mt-6 font-semibold">PostHog — product analytics</h3>
    <p class="mt-2 text-zinc-700">
      PostHog records which pages are viewed and which links are clicked, along with the
      browser and device making the request and an approximate location derived from the IP
      address. It is what tells us which guides people actually read. Where consent is
      required, PostHog loads only after it is given, and not before.
    </p>

    <h3 class="mt-6 font-semibold">Google AdSense — advertising, and the consent banner</h3>
    <p class="mt-2 text-zinc-700">
      Ads on this site are served by Google AdSense, which sets its own cookies and uses
      them to choose and measure ads. The consent banner is also Google's: it is a
      Google-certified consent management platform, and the choice you make in it is the
      signal the rest of this page refers to.
    </p>

    <h3 class="mt-6 font-semibold">GetYourGuide — affiliate links</h3>
    <p class="mt-2 text-zinc-700">
      Some destination pages carry a GetYourGuide widget listing tours and activities. If
      you book through one, Ethos earns a commission at no extra cost to you, and
      GetYourGuide sets cookies to attribute the booking. Where consent is required, the
      widget loads only after it is given.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="mt-10">
    <h2 class="font-semibold">Cookies Ethos sets itself</h2>
    <p class="mt-3 text-zinc-700">
      Two, both first-party, neither used for advertising.
    </p>
    <ul class="mt-3 space-y-2 text-zinc-700">
      <li>
        A <strong>session</strong>
        cookie, which carries the flash messages you see after an action and, if you are
        signed in, the token identifying you. It is cleared when you log out.
      </li>
      <li>
        A <strong>remember</strong>
        me cookie, valid for 60 days, which keeps you signed in between visits. It is set
        only if you ask for it while logging in, and only for accounts. A reader who never
        signs in never receives one.
      </li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section class="mt-10">
    <h2 class="font-semibold">What the site records on its own servers</h2>
    <p class="mt-3 text-zinc-700">
      Each guide keeps a count of how many times it has been read. It is a single number on
      the guide, not a record of who read it.
    </p>
    <p class="mt-3 text-zinc-700">
      If you make an account, Ethos stores the email address and username you give it, the
      guides you write, and the comments and suggestions you leave. Deleting an account or
      its contents is a request to the site owner.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="mt-10">
    <h2 class="font-semibold">Changing your mind</h2>
    <p class="mt-3 text-zinc-700">
      The consent banner can be reopened, and the choice you make in it replaces the one
      before. Refusing after having accepted stops the scripts loading on your next page
      view; it does not reach back and delete what was already collected.
    </p>
    <p class="mt-3 text-zinc-700">
      Every cookie described here, first-party and third-party alike, can be cleared in
      your browser's settings, and your browser can be told to refuse them.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="mt-10">
    <h2 class="font-semibold">Contact</h2>
    <p class="mt-3 text-zinc-700">
      For a question about any of this, or to ask what is held about you, contact the site
      owner.
    </p>
  </section>

  <p class="mt-10 text-sm text-zinc-500">Last updated 6 September 2026.</p>
</div>
